// Lesson 3.3a — Fire Rate: Cooldowns & Time
// The rate-limiting problem: comparing timer functions in Unity (Time.time vs countdown timer vs Coroutines).
// Implementing the compound conditional cooldown in TurretController.cs.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.3a — Fire Rate: Cooldowns &amp; Time</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    In the previous lab, stepping in range triggered a continuous laser of 60 bullets every second.
    In software development, rate limiting is a fundamental problem: actions must not happen faster
    than a designated cadence. Today, you evaluate three different Unity timing functions and build
    a cooldown timer into <code>TurretController.cs</code>.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        How do we make an object "wait" between actions in Unity?
        Because <code>Update()</code> executes every frame without stopping, we cannot use a blocking
        sleep function (which would freeze the entire game window!).
        Instead, game engines offer three common patterns for timing:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Timing Approach</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>How It Works</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>1. <code>Time.time</code> Timestamp</strong></td>
            <td><code>if (Time.time &gt;= lastTime + cooldown)</code></td>
            <td>Records the absolute game time when an action occurred. Checks if the clock has advanced enough since the timestamp.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>2. Countdown Variable</strong></td>
            <td><code>timer -= Time.deltaTime;</code></td>
            <td>Decrements a float variable each frame. Fires when the counter reaches zero, then resets.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>3. Coroutines</strong></td>
            <td><code>yield return new WaitForSeconds(cooldown);</code></td>
            <td>Pauses a method execution asynchronously and resumes after a specified number of seconds without blocking the engine.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Evaluating Timing Approaches</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why is the <code>Time.time</code> timestamp method particularly clean for single, independent actions like firing a shot or checking player damage?</li>
            <li>In what scenarios are <strong>Coroutines</strong> significantly more powerful than checking timestamps in <code>Update()</code>? (Hint: Imagine designing a boss attack that charges a laser for 1.5s, fires 5 rapid shots 0.2s apart, and then rests for 3s.)</li>
            <li>What happens if you use a local variable inside <code>Update()</code> for a timer instead of a class field? Why must timers live as member variables of the class?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — Declare Cooldown Variables.</strong> Open <code>TurretController.cs</code>.
    Add these fields near the top of the class:</p>
    <pre><code>    [SerializeField] private float attackCooldown = 2f;
    private float lastAttackTime = 0f;</code></pre>

    <p>Notice the roles:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>attackCooldown</code> has <code>[SerializeField]</code>: it is a designer tuning knob (wait 2 seconds between shots).</li>
        <li><code>lastAttackTime</code> is private and hidden: it is internal memory tracking when the turret last shot.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — Implement the Compound Condition.</strong> In <code>Update()</code>,
    combine the distance check and the cooldown check using the logical AND operator (<code>&amp;&amp;</code>):</p>
    <pre><code>    private void Update()
    {
        if (playerTransform == null) return;

        float distanceToPlayer = Vector3.Distance(transform.position, playerTransform.position);

        // Both conditions must be true: player in range AND cooldown has passed
        if (distanceToPlayer &lt;= maxAttackRange &amp;&amp; Time.time &gt;= lastAttackTime + attackCooldown)
        {
            AttackPlayer();
            lastAttackTime = Time.time; // Reset the timestamp
        }
    }</code></pre>

    <p>
        Save and return to Unity. Press Play and walk inside the turret's attack radius.
        Instead of a solid wall of bullets, the turret now fires a single projectile, waits exactly 2 seconds,
        and then fires again!
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Timer Faults</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if you comment out the line <code>lastAttackTime = Time.time;</code>? Why does the turret revert back to firing 60 bullets per second?</li>
            <li>In the Inspector for Turret, change <code>Attack Cooldown</code> to <code>0.5</code>. Test it in play mode. How does the threat level change?</li>
            <li>What happens if you initialize <code>private float lastAttackTime = 100f;</code> in code? When the game starts, how many seconds must you wait before the turret can take its very first shot?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Testing the Countdown Timer Alternative</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In <code>TurretController.cs</code>, try implementing the second approach:
            <pre><code>private float timer = 0f;

private void Update()
{
    timer -= Time.deltaTime;
    if (distanceToPlayer &lt;= maxAttackRange &amp;&amp; timer &lt;= 0f)
    {
        AttackPlayer();
        timer = attackCooldown;
    }
}</code></pre>
            Does the turret behave identically in play mode? Compare both code snippets: which one feels more natural to your personal mental model of time?</li>
            <li>How could you use this exact same cooldown pattern to prevent the player from taking damage from spikes 60 times a second if they stand on them?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Compare three timing patterns in Unity (<code>Time.time</code>, countdown timers, Coroutines)</li>
            <li>Construct a compound boolean conditional using <code>&amp;&amp;</code></li>
            <li>Explain why the timestamp reset line (<code>lastAttackTime = Time.time</code>) is mandatory</li>
            <li>Tune a cooldown knob in the Inspector to balance game difficulty</li>
            <li>Commit your fire rate cooldown system to GitHub</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/index.html">Unit 3: The Turret</a> — cooldowns and attack logic in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Time APIs and logical operators</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="3.4a.html">3.4a — Turret Damage, Tuning &amp; Playtest</a></p>
</div>`;
